Malik, **the clause supports your position, but it does not by itself conclusively bar liquidation**.

Its apparent effect is:

- If the halt qualifies as an **“exchange-mandated pricing halt or suspension of quoting”**;
- and the margin deficiency arose from a **mark-to-market adjustment based on that halted pricing**;
- then the Cure Period does not expire until **24 hours after normal quotation resumes**.

That strongly suggests Apex cannot treat that particular deficiency as **uncured** while the halt remains active. But whether it also prevents liquidation depends on the rest of the agreement—especially whether Apex may liquidate:

1. only after the Cure Period expires;
2. immediately upon a margin deficiency, notwithstanding any cure period;
3. under separate default, risk-protection, “deemed insecure,” market-disruption, or emergency provisions; or
4. when quoting is halted by an **ATS**, rather than mandated by an “exchange.” An ATS halt may not satisfy that precise condition unless it resulted from an exchange-wide or regulatory mandate.

So the defensible formulation is:

> **Apex should not be able to force liquidation solely because of the covered margin deficiency before the extended Cure Period expires, provided the ATS halt falls within the clause’s definition.** The excerpt alone does not establish a blanket prohibition on liquidation under every other contractual right.

Given the time pressure, notify Apex in writing immediately that:  
- Section 9.3 applies;  
- the Cure Period remains open;  
- liquidation based on that deficiency would be premature; and  
- Avenir reserves all contractual and legal rights.

Also get transaction counsel to check the definitions and liquidation/default sections urgently. The crucial language is likely in “Cure Period,” “Event of Default,” “Margin,” “Market Disruption,” and Apex’s liquidation or close-out rights.
